{
  "chapter.3F42AE98B7586A25.title": "07 • Super Factory Manager: Program a Factory",
  "task.694CCD813BB87480.title": "I understand this reference",
  "quest.4EF7F4D3881BB980.title": "Start here: Super Factory Manager",
  "quest.4EF7F4D3881BB980.quest_subtitle": "Start Here",
  "quest.4EF7F4D3881BB980.quest_desc": [
    "Super Factory Manager moves items and other resources through a cable network according to a readable program. Labels describe inventories, and timed rules decide what moves, where it goes and how much stays behind. It is especially useful when one complicated production line would otherwise need many filters.",
    "",
    "{image:customatm:textures/quests/sfm.png width:330 height:150 align:center}",
    "",
    "&b&lChoose a branch&r",
    "• First Program",
    "• Item Processing",
    "• Resource Channels",
    "• Factory Projects",
    "",
    "Your route: manager and labels -> a two-chest transfer -> filters and limits -> a furnace line -> diagnostics and a maintainable factory.",
    "",
    "&eStart with the first equipment milestone, then follow the branch you need."
  ],
  "task.76DC6B9ABE38FD5F.title": "I understand this reference",
  "quest.4A7B300A42FB222F.title": "Map legend & completion rules",
  "quest.4A7B300A42FB222F.quest_subtitle": "Reference",
  "quest.4A7B300A42FB222F.quest_desc": [
    "Follow a branch that solves your next problem. Equipment icons lead to concrete inventory milestones; the book card is a reference, and a large diamond marks a workshop project.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Read the setup before crafting. Item milestones detect your inventory without consuming items. Their practical checkmarks are optional reminders; final workshop projects require a practical confirmation. An item in your bag does not prove that a machine is configured. Existing equipment counts: do not rebuild a working system just for this map.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "JEI shows this pack's current recipes. Branches explain how the systems connect. Optional side projects are independent of the main route; no casino win is required."
  ],
  "quest.50469B0A1FB3B1AD.title": "The manager and its program",
  "quest.50469B0A1FB3B1AD.quest_subtitle": "First Program",
  "quest.50469B0A1FB3B1AD.quest_desc": [
    "The Manager executes the program stored on a disk. Cables connect accessible inventories; the program supplies the transfer rules.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Make a Manager, Program Disk, Inventory Cable and Label Gun. Place the Manager near two ordinary chests and connect both with cable. Open its interface and locate the disk editor and examples.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Start with ordinary chests. Machine-side restrictions introduce a second problem before you have learned the language."
  ],
  "task.41430FDF43977C7C.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.1758CCC36719D7CB.title": "Name the endpoints",
  "quest.1758CCC36719D7CB.quest_subtitle": "First Program",
  "quest.1758CCC36719D7CB.quest_desc": [
    "A label is a name the program uses to select network endpoints. A typo or missing label can make correct-looking code select nothing.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Use the Label Gun controls shown in its tooltip to label one chest input and the other output. Apply/synchronize its labels to the Manager, then inspect the network to confirm both names.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use short, distinct names. When copying a setup, verify the Manager has the labels rather than assuming the gun alone configured it."
  ],
  "task.00D9A9E45A80EDD2.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.119971C8963166B4.title": "Your first working program",
  "quest.119971C8963166B4.quest_subtitle": "First Program",
  "quest.119971C8963166B4.quest_desc": [
    "A timed block repeatedly gathers candidate resources with INPUT and transfers them with OUTPUT. INPUT alone does not move items into a hidden buffer.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Put a few cobblestone in input. Enter the example below, save the disk and run it. Confirm the cobblestone appears in output, then stop the program and move it back.",
    "",
    "&6&lWorkshop notes&r",
    "WIRING: Connect the Manager and both chests to the same Inventory Cable network. With the Label Gun, give the source the exact label input and the destination output. Labels belong to blocks; naming a chest with an anvil is not the same operation.",
    "",
    "PROGRAM FLOW: INPUT chooses resources from matching labeled inventories. OUTPUT chooses their destination. Save the disk in the Manager and check compilation before expecting movement. Stop the program, change one label, and observe the error/no-transfer symptom so you can recognize it later.",
    "",
    "",
    "&b&lExample program&r",
    "NAME \"Two chest transfer\"",
    "EVERY 20 TICKS DO",
    "  INPUT FROM \"input\"",
    "  OUTPUT TO \"output\"",
    "END",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "20 ticks is about one second at normal server speed. An empty source or full destination is not necessarily a syntax error.",
    "",
    "{image:customatm:textures/quests/sfm.png width:330 height:150 align:center}"
  ],
  "task.1B82C9393CD95E65.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.7D17FF2200A2F2B7.title": "Read errors before changing hardware",
  "quest.7D17FF2200A2F2B7.quest_subtitle": "First Program",
  "quest.7D17FF2200A2F2B7.quest_desc": [
    "The editor can catch malformed syntax, while execution diagnostics reveal rules that select nothing or cannot transfer.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Read the Manager's error/status display. Deliberately change input to an unused label, observe the result, then restore it. Compare that problem with a missing END in the editor.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Change one thing at a time. A successful parse only proves the language is valid; it cannot guarantee your chest contains the requested item."
  ],
  "task.58E2E64B957D326A.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.6D5CB633FA418D5C.title": "Filter by exact item",
  "quest.6D5CB633FA418D5C.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.6D5CB633FA418D5C.quest_desc": [
    "Explicit identifiers prevent a broad transfer from emptying supplies that belong elsewhere.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Change the input line to INPUT minecraft:cobblestone FROM \"input\". Put cobblestone and dirt together in the source and verify only cobblestone moves.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use the actual registry identifier from advanced tooltips or the network inspector. Display names and translated item names are not identifiers."
  ],
  "task.4C245D9170C47BAD.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.660B3BD35352E197.title": "Keep a reserve",
  "quest.660B3BD35352E197.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.660B3BD35352E197.quest_desc": [
    "Limits control the amount selected or delivered. A reserve prevents automation from taking every ingredient you need by hand.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Open the bundled limits example and compare INPUT 5 RETAIN 3 minecraft:cobblestone FROM \"input\" with your unrestricted rule. Test a source of ten cobblestone and observe several cycles.",
    "",
    "&6&lWorkshop notes&r",
    "RESERVE IS A LOCATION: Decide whether you want to retain stock in the source or maintain a target in the destination. Those are different rules. A factory that needs 64 cobble for itself must not export all 64 just because a trade chest can accept them.",
    "",
    "EXERCISE: Begin with 80 cobble in the source and an empty destination. Use a 64-item source retention rule, run it, and check that 16 moved while 64 remained. Then add 10 and verify only those 10 become surplus.",
    "",
    "",
    "&b&lExample program&r",
    "NAME \"Keep a building reserve\"",
    "EVERY 20 TICKS DO",
    "  INPUT RETAIN 64 minecraft:cobblestone FROM input",
    "  OUTPUT TO output",
    "END",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "An input limit applies per execution, not forever. RETAIN protects a stock level; a transfer of five repeated every second can still drain the rest."
  ],
  "task.5BA2009093529903.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.70159CC525975883.title": "Separate unrelated jobs with FORGET",
  "quest.70159CC525975883.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.70159CC525975883.quest_desc": [
    "Within a trigger, selected resources remain available to later statements. FORGET clears that selection before another independent transfer.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Read the bundled furnace_manager example and identify each FORGET between ingredients, fuel and results. Add a second independent transfer to your chest test, separated by FORGET.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "If a later output receives an unexpected earlier ingredient, inspect selection lifetime before adding more filters."
  ],
  "task.191D06CE07194226.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.3B2C8E2A3D6CB5F9.title": "Respect machine faces",
  "quest.3B2C8E2A3D6CB5F9.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.3B2C8E2A3D6CB5F9.quest_desc": [
    "Many machines expose different inventory slots from different sides. A cable connection does not make every slot available from every direction.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Connect a vanilla furnace, an ore chest, a fuel chest and a result chest. Label them feed, fuel, furnace and results. Read the slots_and_sides example before attempting a transfer.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "For a vanilla furnace, top accepts smelting ingredients; bottom can extract results and accepts fuel through its exposed fuel slot. Modded machines may behave differently."
  ],
  "task.58B68DA4630CE865.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.5BAD5A0D9DCBBFAB.title": "Run a small furnace line",
  "quest.5BAD5A0D9DCBBFAB.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.5BAD5A0D9DCBBFAB.quest_desc": [
    "This program maintains a small ingredient buffer, supplies fuel and removes results without mixing the three jobs.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Use the program below with the labels from the previous step. Add a few raw iron and coal, run it, then verify iron ingots reach results. Keep the output chest visible.",
    "",
    "&6&lWorkshop notes&r",
    "VANILLA FURNACE FACES: The top accepts smelting inputs; horizontal faces accept fuel; the bottom exposes output and fuel-container handling. Configure this demonstration with coal and keep the fuel insertion on a horizontal face.",
    "",
    "TEST THREE STAGES: First deliver one raw iron to the top. Next supply coal from a side. Finally pull the ingot from the bottom into results. Only combine the rules after every stage works. FORGET separates the remembered resources for each independent job.",
    "",
    "",
    "&b&lExample program&r",
    "NAME \"Workshop furnace\"",
    "EVERY 20 TICKS DO",
    "  INPUT FROM feed",
    "  OUTPUT 8 TO EACH furnace TOP SIDE",
    "  FORGET",
    "  INPUT minecraft:coal FROM fuel",
    "  OUTPUT RETAIN 1 TO EACH furnace NORTH SIDE",
    "  FORGET",
    "  INPUT FROM furnace BOTTOM SIDE",
    "  OUTPUT TO results",
    "END",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use plain coal for the first experiment. Fuel-container leftovers such as buckets deserve a separate tested rule."
  ],
  "task.12D7804E111DA741.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.715DE1057A2E5E50.title": "Scale to several machines",
  "quest.715DE1057A2E5E50.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.715DE1057A2E5E50.quest_desc": [
    "A shared label can represent several machines. EACH changes whether a quantity applies separately to each endpoint.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Connect a second furnace under the same label. Compare distribution with the bundled limits and round_robin examples, then choose whether both furnaces should hold a small reserve or receive work in rotation.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Do not assume the first matching inventory is the only target. Inspect how many endpoints carry a shared label before using a large transfer."
  ],
  "task.2D8EDDF589B735C2.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.1BD6617C08FB567D.title": "Inspect the live network",
  "quest.1BD6617C08FB567D.quest_subtitle": "First Program",
  "quest.1BD6617C08FB567D.quest_desc": [
    "The Network Tool reveals connections and endpoint details that are hard to see through walls or machine casings.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Inspect one working chest and one machine with the tool. Locate its inventory inspection controls in the tooltip, then compare exposed slots and resource types from two sides.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use this evidence to choose sides and identifiers. Guessing slot numbers from the GUI can fail because internal inventory numbering differs."
  ],
  "task.373EB43C3BA2CA81.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.2AD403F667C43882.title": "Tags and families of ingredients",
  "quest.2AD403F667C43882.quest_subtitle": "Item Processing",
  "quest.2AD403F667C43882.quest_desc": [
    "Tags let one rule match a compatible family such as a category of ingots. This is useful only if the receiving recipe accepts that family too.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Read the bundled tag_matching and filtering examples. Adapt one to a harmless chest sorting test, and verify both an intended match and an item that should stay behind.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "A broad tag can include valuable variants. Test with small quantities and inspect matches before connecting bulk storage."
  ],
  "task.5DF693BB81820B27.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.2100DDCF4B05190F.title": "Fluids are a separate resource type",
  "quest.2100DDCF4B05190F.quest_subtitle": "Resource Channels",
  "quest.2100DDCF4B05190F.quest_desc": [
    "SFM can move supported resources besides items. A fluid rule needs fluid endpoints and the fluid resource type; an item rule will not move the contents of a tank.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Connect two tanks and consult the bundled resource_types example. Test INPUT fluid::water FROM water_source followed by OUTPUT fluid:: TO water_destination inside a timed block.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Label the tanks exactly as written. Verify exposed tank faces and available destination capacity before increasing the amount."
  ],
  "task.250080817E1744FD.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.653C7C626AC9DC49.title": "Energy and other integrations",
  "quest.653C7C626AC9DC49.quest_subtitle": "Resource Channels",
  "quest.653C7C626AC9DC49.quest_desc": [
    "Supported resource types depend on the installed integrations. The live example and inspector tell you what this build can access.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Open resource_types in the installed Manager and inspect its generated resource-type list. Choose an available energy integration and build a tiny source-to-buffer test using its exact syntax.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Copy the installed example's type name rather than guessing from a web tutorial for another SFM version. Keep power distribution independent from item-selection state."
  ],
  "task.25E6D026410784FC.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.51859F2558C202FC.title": "Pause on purpose",
  "quest.51859F2558C202FC.quest_subtitle": "Resource Channels",
  "quest.51859F2558C202FC.quest_desc": [
    "A production line should be controllable when storage is full, ingredients are scarce or you want to work on the machine.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Read the bundled redstone_signals and if_statements examples. Add one simple condition or use the Manager's run control to stop your furnace line, and confirm it resumes cleanly.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Do not build a complicated condition until the unconditional transfer works. A stopped trigger and a failed transfer require different fixes."
  ],
  "task.56ECCA65AF34D6E1.title": "I understand this reference",
  "quest.1606B6261DA0E892.title": "Troubleshoot in a fixed order",
  "quest.1606B6261DA0E892.quest_subtitle": "Factory Projects",
  "quest.1606B6261DA0E892.quest_desc": [
    "Most silent failures come from labels, resource matching, exposed sides or destination capacity.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Check parse status, trigger timing, endpoint labels, source contents, filters, side/slot access and output space in that order. Temporarily reduce the line to one input and one output to isolate the fault.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Keep a working copy of your program outside the disk before major edits. Comments and descriptive labels save more time than clever compressed code."
  ],
  "task.65E732D8D478A9FF.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.77E937AE6C85076E.title": "Project: a readable processing cell",
  "quest.77E937AE6C85076E.quest_subtitle": "Factory Projects",
  "quest.77E937AE6C85076E.quest_desc": [
    "A good SFM program is a small operating manual for your factory, not just a transfer that happened to work once.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Run two furnaces with filtered input, retained fuel and a separate output. Demonstrate empty input, full output and restart behavior, then add comments explaining each block and save a backup copy.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use slower triggers for ordinary storage work. Increase frequency only when observation shows the transfer interval is the actual bottleneck."
  ],
  "task.361E416577EBE406.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.1E0BD7FB8732B868.title": "Build a network you can see",
  "quest.1E0BD7FB8732B868.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • First Program",
  "quest.1E0BD7FB8732B868.quest_desc": [
    "Inventory Cable gives the Manager access to connected inventories. It does not move items merely because it touches them; the program selects transfers.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Lay a short visible cable run connecting the Manager and two chests. Use the Network Tool to inspect it before hiding cables in walls. Put a few cheap items in only one chest.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "A visible test network separates wiring faults from program faults. Keep the first labels distinct from your main factory."
  ],
  "task.62F82F7A4B290FF7.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.686CC50A3FB1D899.title": "Only the named item moves",
  "quest.686CC50A3FB1D899.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • Item Processing",
  "quest.686CC50A3FB1D899.quest_desc": [
    "An exact item filter protects unrelated stock from a general INPUT statement. The item ID matters more than the translated display name.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Put iron and cobble in the source. Run an INPUT rule filtered to minecraft:iron_ingot and send its result to the destination. Verify iron moved and cobble stayed.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Use the installed disk editor examples for grammar. After the test, widen the filter only if the destination can accept every newly matched item."
  ],
  "task.296EC522BD840197.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.397FEC886580908B.title": "Feed three furnaces fairly",
  "quest.397FEC886580908B.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • Item Processing",
  "quest.397FEC886580908B.quest_desc": [
    "A shared label can refer to several machines. A rule that fills the first available inventory may leave later machines idle, while an EACH rule can distribute a bounded amount to each.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Give three furnaces the same label. Start with an output limit of a few items to EACH furnace, supply fuel separately, and compare their input slots. Then collect all finished outputs into one results chest.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Equal input is not the same as equal processing speed. Compare identical machines before diagnosing distribution behavior."
  ],
  "task.015DC97409ACBEE7.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.25C87C8AD47F80FC.title": "Prove fluid direction",
  "quest.25C87C8AD47F80FC.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • Resource Channels",
  "quest.25C87C8AD47F80FC.quest_desc": [
    "Fluid movement uses a fluid resource identifier and compatible tank capabilities. An item rule targeting a water bucket does not establish that water itself is being transferred.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Use ordinary water and two compatible tanks. Inspect the bundled fluid example, substitute the labels of your test tanks, then transfer a small amount and compare both tank volumes.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Keep item and fluid stages separate with clear labels. Never start this lesson with a fluid you cannot afford to lose."
  ],
  "task.29E1854BB51C1CC5.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.763B9729861706C3.title": "Pause at a known boundary",
  "quest.763B9729861706C3.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • Resource Channels",
  "quest.763B9729861706C3.quest_desc": [
    "An ordinary barrel can give a network a deliberate staging point. It makes a multi-step job easier to inspect than a direct transfer between hidden machine inventories.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Add the buffer to a small test route, label it and send only a small item batch through it. Stop the program halfway and identify exactly where the batch waits, then resume.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "A buffer is finite. Decide how the program behaves when the next machine is full instead of treating extra storage as an unlimited fix."
  ],
  "task.497790DB766F942C.title": "I completed the setup and observed the result",
  "quest.40D17FD06B57866A.title": "A complete batch, accounted for",
  "quest.40D17FD06B57866A.quest_subtitle": "Workshop • Factory Projects",
  "quest.40D17FD06B57866A.quest_desc": [
    "A finished processing cell should move a known batch from input to finished storage and then settle idle without recirculating the product.",
    "",
    "&b&lGet it working&r",
    "Feed enough raw iron for a 32-ingot demonstration, maintain a separate fuel reserve, and run the cell. Count the finished ingots, inspect for leftovers, then run it with no new input and confirm nothing circulates.",
    "",
    "&e&lTip&r",
    "Account for the actual furnace recipe rather than assuming every modded ore-processing recipe is one input to one output."
  ]
}
